name: aglio e olio (kenji's, with fried shallots)
source: https://youtu.be/6PfG_GMFlJg
uses:
  - carbs/pasta
yield: serves 2-3
notes: |
  J. Kenji López-Alt, "The Magic of Fried Shallots (and a Simple Aglio e Olio to Show Them Off)".
  The shallots are the point — the pasta is the delivery mechanism. Written for his NYT column on
  fried shallots, and the full quantities sit behind the NYT paywall, so the amounts below are
  standard aglio e olio proportions built around his described method rather than his exact
  numbers. The technique is his; check the video or NYT if you want the numbers verbatim.

  Two things make it work. First, half the fried shallots go in during the toss and soften into
  the sauce rather than staying crisp — that's what gives it body and a savoury backbone plain
  aglio e olio doesn't have. The other half go on at the end for the crunch. Second, the sauce is
  an emulsion of olive oil and starchy pasta water, and it only forms under vigorous agitation
  over heat. Undertoss it and you get oily pasta with garlic on it.

  Use dried pasta here, not fresh — see the note in the components below.
components:
  - name: fried shallots
    notes: |
      Make far more than you need. They keep 2 weeks airtight at room temp and the oil is the
      real prize — shallot-infused, and it goes into the sauce.
    ingredients:
      - item: shallots
        mass: 250
        note: sliced thin and evenly — a mandoline is worth it, uneven slices burn and undercook in the same pan
      - item: neutral oil
        qty: 500
        unit: ml
        note: enough to cover. canola, peanut, rice bran
    steps:
      - put the sliced shallots and cold oil in a saucepan together and bring up to heat from cold — starting cold drives the water off gradually instead of flash-frying the outside
      - cook at a steady moderate bubble, stirring, until the bubbling slows right down and they turn pale gold. 15-25 mins
      - pull them at pale gold, not brown. they carry over hard and go bitter if you take them to the colour you actually want in the bowl
      - strain, spread on paper towel, salt lightly. they crisp as they cool
      - keep the oil
  - name: pasta and sauce
    ingredients:
      - item: dried spaghetti
        mass: 250
        basis: true
        note: or linguine. dried, not fresh
      - item: garlic
        qty: 6
        unit: clove
        note: sliced thin, not crushed or minced — you want it to stay in identifiable pieces
      - item: extra virgin olive oil
        mass: 60
        note: substitute some of the reserved shallot oil for more of the same flavour
      - item: chilli flakes
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
        note: peperoncino. to taste
      - item: flat leaf parsley
        note: chopped, a good handful
      - item: fried shallots
        note: half into the toss, half on top
      - item: pecorino or parmesan
        optional: true
        note: not traditional in aglio e olio, and it competes with the shallots. leave it off the first time
    steps:
      - boil the pasta in well-salted water, pulling it a good minute shy of al dente — it finishes in the pan
      - meanwhile, warm the olive oil in a wide skillet over medium-low and add the sliced garlic
      - cook the garlic gently to pale golden. low and slow — browned garlic is bitter and there's nowhere in a four-ingredient sauce for it to hide
      - add the chilli flakes and swirl for 30 secs off the direct heat
      - add the drained pasta, a good splash of pasta water, and half the fried shallots
      - toss hard over high heat until the water and oil come together into a glossy sauce that coats the strands. add more pasta water as it tightens. this takes a solid minute of real agitation
      - off the heat, stir through the parsley
      - plate and pile the remaining fried shallots on top
  - name: why dried pasta
    notes: |
      This is a southern Italian dish and it's a dried-pasta idiom top to bottom. Three reasons
      fresh egg pasta is the wrong call:

      Egg. The whole dish is garlic, oil and chilli — austere by design. Egg dough is rich and
      eggy and it blurs that. There's nothing for the shallots to stand out against.

      Starch and time. The emulsion runs on starch shed into the water and picked up off the
      pasta's surface during the toss. Dried durum pasta sheds that starch steadily over 9-10
      mins and comes out of the pot firm enough to survive a hard minute of tossing. Fresh pasta
      is done in 90 secs and softens fast — you're trying to build an emulsion against a clock,
      with pasta that's shredding while you do it.

      Surface. Bronze-die dried spaghetti is rough and grabs sauce. A rolled fresh sheet is
      smooth by comparison, and the sauce slides.

      If you want it fresh anyway, use the eggless semolina-and-water dough from carbs/fresh-pasta
      as spaghetti alla chitarra or pici — no egg, durum, firm enough to toss. That's a legitimate
      dish. Fresh egg tagliatelle under aglio e olio is not.
